\\\\  & =⟨-4\\pi (2),4\\pi (\\frac{1}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8\\pi ,\\,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-352565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972600df0dffcfaef07aafeba28ce4b275ca8b2195529bf6eb8b68c636aa406a"} {"question" "q-352565" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-352565" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8\\pi"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-352565" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-352565" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-121724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨6t-1,{3}^{t+1}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-121724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({3}^{t+1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,{3}^{t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=1,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨6,{3}^{1+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,{3}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,9\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-121724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972600df0dffcfaef07aafeba28ce4b275ca8b2195529bf6eb8b68c636aa406a"} {"question" "q-121724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-121724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(1)=⟨5,9\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩"] ["math" "v(1)=⟨5,9⟩"] ["math" "v(1)=⟨6,3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩"] ["math" "v(1)=⟨6,9\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩"] ["math" "v(1)=⟨6,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-121724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(1)=⟨6,9\\operatorname{ln}⁡3⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-7152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,2{t}^{2}+9⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=e.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-7152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+9)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t,4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=e,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(e) & =⟨2e\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+e,4e⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3e,4e⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=e$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(e),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(e)| & =\\sqrt{(3e)^{2}+(4e)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =5e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-7152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972600df0dffcfaef07aafeba28ce4b275ca8b2195529bf6eb8b68c636aa406a"} {"question" "q-7152" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-7152" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5\\sqrt{5e}"] ["math" "5\\sqrt{e}"] ["math" "5e"] ["math" "5e\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "7e"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-7152" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5e"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-7151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2},\\,3t+7⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-7151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+7)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=4,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nv=⟨4,3⟩\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=4$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(4)| & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed when $t=4$ is $5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-7151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972600df0dffcfaef07aafeba28ce4b275ca8b2195529bf6eb8b68c636aa406a"} {"question" "q-7151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-7151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-7151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-347189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),\\,\\frac{3}{t}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed $|v|$ of the particle at time $t=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe speed at time $t=3$ is\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-347189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t+1},\\,-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=3,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨\\frac{1}{3+1},\\,-\\frac{3}{{3}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=3$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(3)| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{144}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-347189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972600df0dffcfaef07aafeba28ce4b275ca8b2195529bf6eb8b68c636aa406a"} {"question" "q-347189" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-347189" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{12}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-352587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1),\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed $|v|$ of the particle at time $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe speed at time $t=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-352587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{2t+1},\\,\\frac{1}{t+1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=1,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\frac{2}{2(1)+1},\\,\\frac{1}{1+1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=1$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(1)| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-352587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972600df0dffcfaef07aafeba28ce4b275ca8b2195529bf6eb8b68c636aa406a"} {"question" "q-352587" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-352587" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-352583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{{4}^{t-1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4},\\frac{{9}^{t-1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=1.$\n\nThe speed at time $t=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-352583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{4}^{t-1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{9}^{t-1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{4}^{t-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4},\\frac{{9}^{t-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{4}^{t-1},{9}^{t-1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=1,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨{4}^{1-1},{9}^{1-1}⟩=⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=1$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(1)| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-352583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972600df0dffcfaef07aafeba28ce4b275ca8b2195529bf6eb8b68c636aa406a"} {"question" "q-352583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-352583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-51304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{2}{e}^{-2t},\\,(t-1)^{3}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-51304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}{e}^{-2t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t{e}^{-2t}-2{t}^{2}{e}^{-2t},3(t-1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t(1-t){e}^{-2t},3(t-1)^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2t(1-t){e}^{-2t},3(t-1)^{2}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t(1-t){e}^{-2t}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0,1, \\\\ 3(t-1)^{2}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=1$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-51304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972600df0dffcfaef07aafeba28ce4b275ca8b2195529bf6eb8b68c636aa406a"} {"question" "q-51304" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-51304" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=0"] ["math" "t=0,1"] ["math" "t=0,2"] ["math" "t=1"] ["text" "The particle is never stationary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-51304" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-121738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3}-3t,{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+9t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-121738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+9t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-3,3{t}^{2}-12t+9⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3({t}^{2}-1),3({t}^{2}-4t+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(t+1)(t-1),3(t-1)(t-3)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨3(t+1)(t-1),3(t-1)(t-3)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(t+1)(t-1)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=\\pm 1, \\\\ 3(t-1)(t-3)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=1,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=1$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-121738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972600df0dffcfaef07aafeba28ce4b275ca8b2195529bf6eb8b68c636aa406a"} {"question" "q-121738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-121738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=0"] ["math" "t=0,1"] ["math" "t=1"] ["math" "t=1,3"] ["text" "The particle is never stationary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-121738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-347191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡((t-1)^{2}+2),(t-1)^{3}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-347191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡((t-1)^{2}+2)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-1)^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2(t-1)}{(t-1)^{2}+2},\\,3(t-1)^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{2(t-1)}{(t-1)^{2}+2},\\,3(t-1)^{2}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(t-1)}{(t-1)^{2}+2}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=1, \\\\ 3(t-1)^{2}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=1$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-347191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972600df0dffcfaef07aafeba28ce4b275ca8b2195529bf6eb8b68c636aa406a"} {"question" "q-347191" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-347191" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-352722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨(t-6)^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}-1},\\,({t}^{2}-36)^{3}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-352722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-6)^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}-1}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(({t}^{2}-36)^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(t-6){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t(t-6)^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}-1},\\,6t({t}^{2}-36)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(t-6)({t}^{2}-6t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1},\\,6t({t}^{2}-36)^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2(t-6)({t}^{2}-6t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1},\\,6t({t}^{2}-36)^{2}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t-6)({t}^{2}-6t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=6,\\text{ }3\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ 6t({t}^{2}-36)^{2}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0,\\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=6$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-352722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972600df0dffcfaef07aafeba28ce4b275ca8b2195529bf6eb8b68c636aa406a"} {"question" "q-352722" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-352722" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-31654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3}-3t,\\,2{t}^{2}+6t-3⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-31654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+6t-3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-3,4t+6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3({t}^{2}-1),2(2t+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(t-1)(t+1),2(2t+3)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨3(t-1)(t+1),2(2t+3)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(t-1)(t+1)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=\\pm 1, \\\\ 2(2t+3)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere is no value of $t$ that satisfies both equations, so there are no stationary points." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-31654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "972600df0dffcfaef07aafeba28ce4b275ca8b2195529bf6eb8b68c636aa406a"} {"question" "q-31654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-31654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=1"] ["math" "t=2"] ["math" "t=3"] ["math" "t=4"] ["text" "The particle is never stationary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "q-31654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The particle is never stationary"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "aa7bd7c741e5c3deb397050d8b46a3549627aaed0824252feb040909ac051c8f"} {"question" "e-616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 3t^3, 2t^2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/example-616.json" "file_sha256" "5c7c8050cce5ad00fab3cc2962f2dfdf333eae1cbb4ccd0af97ffd26597aaa5c"} {"question" "e-616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9{t}^{2},\\,4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/example-616.json" "file_sha256" "5c7c8050cce5ad00fab3cc2962f2dfdf333eae1cbb4ccd0af97ffd26597aaa5c"} {"question" "e-617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 12t, 2t^3\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/example-617.json" "file_sha256" "ec01de610dbde8c4bb7bc1517492214f5c9f395863c3043678b3485652e96a82"} {"question" "e-617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,6{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=1$, we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨12,6(1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/example-617.json" "file_sha256" "ec01de610dbde8c4bb7bc1517492214f5c9f395863c3043678b3485652e96a82"} {"question" "e-618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 6t^2+2, 2t^3\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/example-618.json" "file_sha256" "9843ab9a3a3305df1da2eeee116e23fa9b7ea54438d921e7a3d7c9a6dcc7274d"} {"question" "e-618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12t,6{t}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2$, we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨12(2),6(2)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨24,24⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=2$ we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf{v}(2),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(2)| & =\\sqrt{{24}^{2}+{24}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2({24}^{2})} \\\\  & =24\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed when $t=2$ is $24\\sqrt{2}\\approx 33.94$ to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/example-618.json" "file_sha256" "9843ab9a3a3305df1da2eeee116e23fa9b7ea54438d921e7a3d7c9a6dcc7274d"} {"question" "e-619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle t^2-2t, t^3-3t \\rangle, \\quad t\\in[0,\\infty).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/example-619.json" "file_sha256" "6422104744549b4be3be7a34c1004b4874c0914208fad5bae976efd8c2790053"} {"question" "e-619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,\\,3{t}^{2}-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2t-2,3{t}^{2}-3⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-2 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=1, \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-3 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $t=-1$ is outside of the domain of $t,$ so we discard it. \n\nThe solution $t=1$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only time when the object is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/example-619.json" "file_sha256" "6422104744549b4be3be7a34c1004b4874c0914208fad5bae976efd8c2790053"}] "usage" {"q-7151" [] "e-616" [] "q-31637" [] "e-617" [] "e-619" [] "e-618" [] "q-51304" []} "basis" 1447 "retractions" [[17592186083563 :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving in the $x$-$y$ plane. Its position vector relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by\n\n$r = ⟨t^{3/2} + 4t^{2}, 11t - 1⟩, t > 0$.\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$."] [17592186083833 :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$r = ⟨\\frac{t^{2}}{2}, 3t + 7⟩, t > 0$.\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t = 4$."] [17592186083681 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n$[MATH: r = ⟨t^{2}e^{-2t}, (t - 1)^{3}⟩, t ∈ [0, ∞).]$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary."] [17592186082985 :question/problem "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t > 0$ is given by\n$\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 3t^3, 2t^2\\rangle$.\nCalculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}$."] [17592186082985 :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{dx}{dt}, \\frac{dy}{dt}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(3t^{3}), \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨9t^{2}, 4t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186082986 :question/problem "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t > 0$ is given by\n$\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 12t, 2t^3\\rangle$.\nCalculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t=1$."] [17592186082986 :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{dx}{dt}, \\frac{dy}{dt}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(12t), \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{3})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨12, 6t^{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=1$, we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(1) &= ⟨12, 6(1)^{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨12, 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186082987 :question/problem "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t > 0$ is given by\n$\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 6t^2+2, 2t^3\\rangle$.\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=2$."] [17592186082987 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}$. Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{dx}{dt}, \\frac{dy}{dt}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(6t^{2} + 2), \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{3})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨12t, 6t^{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2$, we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(2) &= ⟨12(2), 6(2)^{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨24, 24⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=2$ we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf{v}(2)$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid v(2) \\mid &= \\sqrt{24^{2} + 24^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2(24^{2})} \\\\\n&= 24\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed when $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n24\\sqrt{2}\\approx 33.94\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."] [17592186082988 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n$[MATH: \\mathbf{r} = \\langle t^2-2t, t^3-3t \\rangle, \\quad t\\in[0,\\infty).]$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary."] [17592186082988 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}$. Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{dx}{dt}, \\frac{dy}{dt}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - 2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3} - 3t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2t - 2, 3t^{2} - 3⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= 0 \\\\\n⟨2t - 2, 3t^{2} - 3⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2t - 2 &= 0⇒t = 1, \\\\\n3t^{2} - 3 &= 0⇒t = ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $t=-1$ is outside of the domain of $t$, so we discard it.\n\nThe solution $t=1$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only time when the object is stationary."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186080755 17592186080756 17592186080757 17592186080758 17592186080759 17592186081453 17592186081454 17592186081455 17592186081456 17592186081457 17592186082365 17592186082366 17592186082367 17592186082368 17592186082369] "immutable_fields" [[17592186080754 :answer-field/correct] [17592186080754 :answer-field/id] [17592186080754 :answer-field/key] [17592186080754 :answer-field/type] [17592186081452 :answer-field/correct] [17592186081452 :answer-field/id] [17592186081452 :answer-field/key] [17592186081452 :answer-field/type] [17592186082364 :answer-field/correct] [1